# Qt / C++ ownership: one owner

Pick **one** owner. Mixing `new`/`delete`, `deleteLater`, a Qt parent, and a default-deleter `unique_ptr` on the same object is how jobs leak or double-delete.

- **Non-QObject heap types** (`Job`, `QTemporaryDir`): `std::unique_ptr`. No destructor `delete`. Store them in `std::vector`, not `QVector` (QList detach copy-constructs).
- **QWidget / parented QObject** (`QLabel`, `QPushButton`, `QAction`, layouts): `makeQtPtr<T>(parent)` (see below). No raw `new`. Members that observe these widgets are `QPointer<T>` (auto-null if Qt deletes first). Do not store a default-deleter `unique_ptr` after the object has a Qt parent.
- **Unparented QObject you uniquely own** (`ClipExporter` on a job, `VideoConcatenator`, `QProcess`): `std::make_unique<Foo>()` with **no** Qt parent. Never also `new Foo(this)`.
- **Transfer across objects**: the setter takes `std::unique_ptr<T>` by value; the caller `std::move`s. Do not pass a raw `T*` into a setter that later `delete`s it.
- **Destructor**: do not `delete` or `reset()` unique_ptr members. They run after the destructor body. `reset()` only when releasing **early** (cancel, replace, dismiss).
- **Destroying a QObject from its own signal**: `release()` then `disconnect()` then `deleteLater()`. Do not `unique_ptr::reset()` on that stack.

```cpp
struct QtParentDeleter {
  void operator()(QObject* object) const noexcept {
    if (object && object->parent() == nullptr) delete object;
  }
};
template<typename T, typename... Args>
std::unique_ptr<T, QtParentDeleter> makeQtPtr(Args&&... args) {
  return {std::make_unique<T>(std::forward<Args>(args)...).release()};
}

// BAD — raw new; member dangles if Qt parent deletes first
titleLabel_ = new QLabel(contentContainer);

// BAD — default unique_ptr AND Qt parent: two owners
std::unique_ptr<QLabel> titleLabel_ = std::make_unique<QLabel>(this);

// GOOD — parent-aware unique_ptr; QPointer only observes
auto titleLabel = makeQtPtr<QLabel>(contentContainer.get());
titleLabel_ = titleLabel.get();
layout->addWidget(titleLabel.get());
```
